# SQLAlchemy

{% if framework == "fastapi" %}
This project's data layer is SQLAlchemy 2.0 in **async** mode
(`sqlalchemy.ext.asyncio`) — every query and session operation is
awaited. This skill covers how *this project* uses it, not the whole
library surface, so new code matches the existing conventions instead
of introducing a second style (in particular: don't add a sync
engine/session anywhere in this codebase — see `references/gotchas.md`
for why that's worse than it sounds).
{% else %}
This project's data layer is SQLAlchemy 2.0 in **sync** mode (plain
`sqlalchemy`, no `asyncio` extension) — Flask's request handling is
sync, so there's no `await` anywhere in the data layer. This skill
covers how *this project* uses it, not the whole library surface, so
new code matches the existing conventions instead of introducing a
second style.
{% endif %}

## When to reach for this

- Adding, changing, or removing a model in `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py`.
- Writing a new query in a route handler.
- Touching `src/{{ package_name }}/core/db.py` — engine, session
  {% if framework == "fastapi" %}factory, or the `get_session` dependency{% else %}factory, or `get_session()`{% endif %}.
{% if framework == "fastapi" %}
- Debugging a "greenlet" error, an unawaited coroutine warning, or a
  handler that looks async but hangs.
{% else %}
- Debugging a session that has stale data, a `DetachedInstanceError`,
  or a session that appears to leak across requests.
{% endif %}

## Quick reference

- **Models**: `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py`. SQLAlchemy 2.0
  declarative style — `class Base(DeclarativeBase): pass`, then each
  model declares columns with `Mapped[...]` type annotations and
  `mapped_column(...)`, not the legacy `Column(...)` + `declarative_base()`
  style:

  ```python
  class Item(Base):
      __tablename__ = "items"

      id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)
      name: Mapped[str]
      description: Mapped[str | None] = mapped_column(default=None)
  ```

  `Mapped[str]` alone (no `mapped_column()`) is a plain required
  column; `Mapped[str | None]` makes it nullable. Reach for
  `mapped_column(...)` only when you need to say more (`primary_key`,
  a default, a `ForeignKey`, an explicit column name).

{% if framework == "fastapi" %}
- **Engine & sessions**: `src/{{ package_name }}/core/db.py`.
  `create_async_engine(settings.database_url, ...)` built once at
  module scope; `async_session_maker = async_sessionmaker(engine,
  class_=AsyncSession, expire_on_commit=False)`. Every request gets its
  own session via the `get_session()` FastAPI dependency — never a
  session held longer than one request, never a session shared across
  requests.
- **Queries**: always `await` — `await session.execute(select(Item))`
  then `.scalars().all()` for a list, `await session.get(Item, id)` for
  a primary-key lookup, `session.add(obj)` (not awaited — it's
  synchronous bookkeeping) then `await session.commit()`.
- **Run it**: `uv run fastapi dev src/{{ package_name }}/main.py` — see
  the `fastapi` skill for the app layer these sessions plug into via
  `Depends(get_session)`.
{% else %}
- **Engine & sessions**: `src/{{ package_name }}/core/db.py`.
  `SessionLocal = scoped_session(sessionmaker())` at module scope
  (unconfigured — no engine yet); `init_db(app)` binds the real engine
  with `SessionLocal.configure(bind=engine)` once the app factory knows
  the database URL, and registers `app.teardown_appcontext(lambda
  exc=None: SessionLocal.remove())` so every request's session is torn
  down automatically. `get_session()` just returns `SessionLocal()` —
  there's no Flask equivalent of FastAPI's `Depends()`, so this is
  called directly inside each route handler, not injected.
- **Queries**: no `await` anywhere — `session.execute(select(Item)).scalars().all()`
  for a list, `session.get(Item, id)` for a primary-key lookup,
  `session.add(obj)` then `session.commit()`.
- **Run it**: `uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py run`
  — see the `flask` skill for the app-factory layer `get_session()`
  plugs into.
{% endif %}

